From b2c781d11cda547ce87b4d34191e8e1ad13225e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bjorn Pagen <bjorn@REPLACE-ME>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 21:25:03 -0500
Subject: [PATCH v1 1/2] btree_gist: Measure varlena penalty at first differing
 byte

gbt_var_penalty() prices adding a key to a subtree, when the key
extends the subtree's bounds without shortening their common prefix,
by the bytes just past the prefix that gbt_var_node_cp_len() computes.
For text and bpchar in a multibyte encoding, gbt_var_node_cp_len()
ended that prefix at a character boundary, so the penalty compared the
leading bytes of the next characters.  In UTF-8 those are shared by
whole ranges of characters: all of Latin-1 Supplement begins with C2 or
C3, and all of the CJK Unified Ideographs with one of E4 to E9.  Adding
such a key therefore cost about the same in every subtree, gistchoose()
placed it with no regard to its order, the leaf pages' key ranges
overlapped, and a point lookup descended into many of them.  A one-row
lookup among 20000 keys of two ideographs read 14 buffers rather than
3.

The character alignment is otherwise dead code, since text-string types
have not truncated internal keys since ef770cbb6.  Remove it, as master
did in b82d69abf6, together with gbtree_vinfo.eml, which only it used.
This changes only where new entries go, so existing indexes remain
valid; new insertions are placed well at once, and an index regains its
full lookup speed when rebuilt.

This is b82d69abf6 back-patched; only its context differs.
